"_____________________________________________________________"
"The )| Pipe Operator in Bern"
"x )| f passes x as the FIRST argument to f, so data flows left to right."
"_____________________________________________________________\n"
import core

"[1. Piping into a function]"
"x )| f is the same as f(x)."
"[3, 1, 2] )| reverse"

"\nResult:"
[3, 1, 2] )| reverse

"\n_____________________________________________________________\n"
"[2. Extra arguments]"
"The piped value becomes the first argument; you supply the rest."
"[1, 2, 3, 4] )| filter(\\x -> x > 2)   is   filter([1, 2, 3, 4], \\x -> x > 2)"

"\nResult:"
[1, 2, 3, 4] )| filter(\x -> x > 2)

"\n_____________________________________________________________\n"
"[3. Chaining pipes]"
"Pipes are left-associative, so you can build a left-to-right pipeline."
"[1, 2, 3, 4] )| filter(\\x -> x % 2 == 0) )| map(\\x -> x * 10)"

"\nResult:"
[1, 2, 3, 4] )| filter(\x -> x % 2 == 0) )| map(\x -> x * 10)

"\n_____________________________________________________________\n"
"[4. Piping into a lambda]"
"You can pipe directly into an anonymous function."
"5 )| (\\x -> x + 1)"

"\nResult:"
5 )| (\x -> x + 1)

"\n_____________________________________________________________\n"
"[5. Precedence]"
"The pipe binds looser than arithmetic, so 2 + 3 is computed first."
"def is_five(n) -> n == 5"
"2 + 3 )| is_five"

"\nResult:"
def is_five(n) -> n == 5
2 + 3 )| is_five
